<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- The ground behind the crescent: FLAT WHITE, like Google's own launcher
     icons on this phone - Drive, Files, Gmail, Google, Maps, Meet, Messages are
     all white tiles with a saturated mark.

     This file used to open "No FLAT colour can serve this artwork", and that
     was true of the colour it was written about. The shipped icon was #12161B,
     a near-black tile: it read the dawn horn at 8.79:1 and the night horn at
     1.79:1, an orange fingernail on black, reported as the darkest icon on the
     launcher. A diagonal gradient followed, light behind the cool horn and deep
     behind the warm one, and it worked.

     White inverts the problem rather than repeating it, and that is what makes
     it viable where black was not. On white the night horn holds 10.15:1 and
     the DAWN horn is the one that gets swallowed, at 2.07:1. The difference is
     that this end can be fixed in the ARTWORK: a dawn orange deepened from tone
     73 to 62 is still unmistakably a dawn orange, whereas the fix black needed
     was to LIGHTEN the night horn, which is to stop it being night. One end of
     this ramp has somewhere to go and the other does not.

     So the warm half of ic_launcher_foreground.xml moved with this file:
     tone 62 #D4814C at the tip, 2.98:1 on white, with the ember stop deepened
     to match. Change one and you must change the other - and note the app's
     own dial keeps the ORIGINAL Arc stops, deliberately. The dial is ~200dp on
     a known ground; the icon is ~40dp on someone's wallpaper.

     Two things measured here that survive the change, both first attempts wrong:

     THE AXIS, if a gradient ever returns. Read off the RENDERED icon, not the
     path data: the cool half of the artwork centres at x=0.35 of the tile and
     the warm half at x=0.63, so any split is very nearly horizontal. Reasoning
     from the crescent's -32 degree rotation gives 45 degrees and puts the light
     in the wrong corner.

     AND A RATIO CANNOT REFEREE THIS ARTWORK ON A TINTED GROUND. Sampled off the
     render, the worst warm-versus-ground pair in the gradient version was
     1.00:1 - orange and blue-grey at identical luminance - in the shipped icon
     as much as in any variant of it. That is the strongest argument for white:
     on white every pair is a real ratio again, and the numbers above mean
     something. -->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp" android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108" android:viewportHeight="108">
    <path
        android:pathData="M0,0 h108 v108 h-108 Z"
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF" />
</vector>
